/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A cannula is inserted into the radial artery at the wrist, sometimes the brachial or femoral artery.
   - The catheter and transducer form a second-order system with a natural frequency and a damping ratio.
   - MAP is mean arterial pressure in mmHg; S is systolic pressure in mmHg; D is diastolic pressure in mmHg.
   - An inflatable rubber bladder inside a cloth cuff is wrapped round the upper arm and pumped above systolic pressure.
   - Phase V is diastolic in most adults.
   - The mercury manometer is a vertical glass tube; cuff pressure pushes mercury up a calibrated scale.
   - Automated oscillometric monitors use the same cuff but no microphone.
*/
