/* book: lead: Cardiac output is the volume of blood the heart pushes out in one minute. Doctors need it to judge whether a sick heart is pumping enough, and to guide drugs and fluids. Dilution methods measure it without opening the chest: inject a known amount of indicator into the blood, then record how the indicator concentration changes downstream. The faster the blood flows, the more the indicator is spread */
