/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Cardiac output (CO) is the blood volume the heart ejects per minute, in litres per minute (L/min).
   - The Fick principle is the reference method: oxygen consumption divided by the arteriovenous oxygen difference gives CO.
   - Inject a known mass I (mg) of indicator into a vein.
   - CO is cardiac output in L/min; I is the mass of indicator injected in mg; A = ∫ c(t) dt is the area under the first-pass concentration-time curve in mg·s/L; 60 converts seconds to minutes.
   - The method assumes complete mixing, no indicator loss before the sensor, and steady flow during the measurement.
   - Dye dilution uses indocyanine green (ICG), a green dye that stays in the blood vessels and is removed by the liver.
   - After about 20–30 s some dye returns through the circulation and creates a second, smaller hump called recirculation.
   - Some ICG preparations contain sodium iodide, so ask about iodine allergy.
   - Thermal dilution replaces the dye with cold fluid and the densitometer with a thermistor.
   - The recorder plots the temperature fall ΔT (°C) against time (s).
*/
