/* book exam traps: Dropping the factor 60 in CO = 60 I / A. With I = 5 mg and A = 60 mg·s/L, dropping 60 gives 0.083 L/s (not L/min), which is the same as 5 L/min. | Including the recirculation hump in A. That makes A too large and CO too small, because CO is inversely proportional to the area. | Mixing time units: the area must be in mg·s/L before multiplying by 60. If the curve is integrated in mg·min/L, do not multiply by 60 again. | Using the wrong computation constant K for thermal dilution when the injectate volume or temperature is changed. The curve can be correct while CO is wrong. */
